<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Dataset generalization results for SPC in PnP and Unrolling. Each <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Unrolling were optimized with CIFAR-10, and tested with the CIFAR-10 and STL10. For each dataset, the best results are highlighted in <span id="S5.T2.fig1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFDFDF;">bold teal</span>, while the second-best results are shown in <span id="S5.T2.fig1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDFBF;">underline orange</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<div id="S5.F5.fig1.2" class="ltx_sectional-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage">
          
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Deep Image Prior</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the deblurring task with a kernel bandwidth of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=4.0</annotation></semantics></math>. We train <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with the Places365 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, where we used 28.000 images for training and 7000 images for testing. All images were resized to <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128\times 128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128\times 128</annotation></semantics></math>. The network <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math> was trained following (<a href="#A1.E15" title="In A.7 Deep Image Prior ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for 1000 iterations. The network <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is a U-Net of the same size as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Fig. <a href="#S5.F5.fig1" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 State-of-the-art comparison ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is shown the reconstruction performance of DIP and NPN-DIP for different values of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The results show significant improvements of up to 5 dB and convergence improvements.</p>
</div>
</section>
        
</div><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01608v1/dip.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/147;" width="215" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Performance of DIP and NPN-DIP for different values of <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Diffusion model solvers</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrated the proposed regularization term into two widely adopted DM frameworks, DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and DiffPIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. Specifically, we evaluated the proposed regularization on the limited-angle CT inverse problem, using 60 out of 180 total views (spaced every 1°) under a parallel-beam geometry.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3.fig1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:120.8pt;">
<p id="S5.T3.fig1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:207.0pt;">We train the DM for 1000 epochs with batch size 4 using the AdamW optimizer and learning rate <math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We used a cosine variance schedule ranging from <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.02</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1000</annotation></semantics></math> time steps. The LoDoPaB-CT dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> was resized to <math id="S5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> and used for training; in testing, we used 10 test set slices. <math id="S5.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a U-Net which was trained for 100 epochs with a learning rate <math id="S5.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of 4 using AdamW. Table <a href="#S5.T3.fig1" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.6 Diffusion model solvers ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the obtained results for different values of <math id="S5.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. For DPS, the NPN regularization consistently improves reconstruction performance by up to 1.85 dB. For DiffPIR, it yields improvements of up to 0.61 dB. Details on the implementation of NPN into DPS and DiffPIR are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS8" title="A.8 Diffusion-based Solvers ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>.</p>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<div id="S5.T3.fig1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:120.8pt;height:67.7pt;vertical-align:-32.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-37.1pt,20.8pt) scale(0.619417124338344,0.619417124338344) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T3.fig1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.fig1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></td>
