</section>
<section id="A1.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.11 </span>Additional Experiments</h3>

<div id="A1.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Tables <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we provide additional results of MRI and deblurring, respectively, using PnP, RED, and sparsity priors for acceleration factors of 4, 8, and 12, and <math id="A1.SS11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of 2,6,10. These results validate the robustness of NPN regularization under different numbers of measurements.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figures <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the effect of the parameter <math id="A1.SS11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on the quality of the recovery is shown, for MRI, Deblurring, and SPC, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ A.11 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendices ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the results in terms of convergence of the PnP and PnP-NPN for SPC with <math id="A1.SS11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m/n=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m/n=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. The color of the line indicates the type of projection matrix used, orthonormal by QR (red) or designed by Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Learning the NPN Prior ‣ 3 Method ‣ NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (blue). The color shade indicates the percentage of the low-dimensional subspace <math id="A1.SS11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p/n</annotation></semantics></math>, ranging from 0.1 to 0.9. The best case is with <math id="A1.SS11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p/n=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p/n=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> designed, which was the one used in the experiments of the main paper.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
