</section>
<section id="A1.SS14" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.14 </span>Comparison with reconstruction model</h3>

<div id="A1.SS14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To create a fair comparison with the proposed method, we consider the network <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the same number of parameters as <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> but is trained to reconstruct directly the underlying signal i.e., <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathrm{W}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x,y}}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathrm{W}(\mathbf{y})\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathrm{W}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x,y}}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathrm{W}(\mathbf{y})\|</annotation></semantics></math>. The network was trained with the same number of epochs as <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> (300 epochs), using the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate two aspects here: i) the reconstruction performance of the network <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and ii) incorporating into the PnP-ADMM the regularization but in the form of <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathrm{W}}(\mathbf{x})=\|\mathbf{Sx}-\mathbf{S}\mathrm{W}^{*}(\mathbf{y})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathrm{W}}(\mathbf{x})=\|\mathbf{Sx}-\mathbf{S}\mathrm{W}^{*}(\mathbf{y})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We trained the network <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> with the Places365 dataset. The PnP-ADMM was set with 100 iterations, using the DnCNN-Lipchitz denoiser. We set <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>. In the following table, we report the obtained results. The results show that the proposed approach overall improves the baseline, the reconstruction of the model <math id="A1.SS14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the regularization based on this model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Comparison of PSNR (dB) and SSIM metrics</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Base PnP-ADMM</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">NPN PnP-ADMM</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">PnP-ADMM with <math id="A1.T11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathrm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathrm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="A1.T11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}^{*}(\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}^{*}(\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T11.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T11.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">PSNR (dB)</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">22.30</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">23.87</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">21.20</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">19.71</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T11.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T11.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">SSIM</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.586</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.678</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.534</td>
<td id="A1.T11.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.490</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the tables below, we show the training and execution time in seconds (s) and minutes (min), respectively. These results are obtained for a batch size of 100 images with a resolution of <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128\times 128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128\times 128</annotation></semantics></math> and 100 iterations of the PnP-ADMM. <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> (reconstruction network from the measurements <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are trained for 300 epochs. The training times of <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are very similar due to having the same number of parameters. Despite a modest increase of 0.20 seconds over the baseline PnP-ADMM, NPN achieves a 1.57 dB PSNR gain. Moreover, compared to PnP-ADMM with <math id="A1.SS14.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathrm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathrm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, NPN is 0.28 seconds faster while delivering a 2.67 dB improvement in PSNR. These results show that the NPN regularization yields substantial quality gains with minimal or favorable time trade-offs.</p>
</div>
